<BaseLayout
  title="Leadership & Track Record — Stefan Papp"
  description="How I lead engineering organizations — and what that has produced. Philosophy, track record, and what people who have worked with me say."
  url="/leadership"
>
  <!-- Hero Banner -->
  <section class="bg-navy pt-24 pb-20 px-4 md:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-white font-sans mb-6 leading-tight">
        Leadership &amp; Track Record
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-white/75 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        My job as an engineering leader is to set direction clearly, hire people better than me, and then mostly get out of the way. The outcomes below are theirs. The conditions that made them possible were mine to create.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Philosophy Section -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 px-4 md:px-8 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy font-sans mb-8">How I think about leadership</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-slate leading-relaxed text-lg">
        <p>
          The single highest-leverage thing an engineering leader can do is hire well and then resist the urge to justify their own position by making decisions that their reports should be making. Great engineers do not want to be told how to solve problems — they want clear context about what matters and why, genuine ownership of the solution space, and an environment where it is safe to be wrong. My job is to create those conditions and then hold myself accountable to the outcomes.
        </p>
        <p>
          I practice radical transparency about strategy, constraints, and trade-offs. That means sharing the reasoning behind decisions, including the uncomfortable ones, and inviting scrutiny rather than managing perception. It also means being honest when I do not know something — which is more often than most leaders are willing to admit. I would rather my teams have an accurate picture of the uncertainty we are operating in than false confidence based on information I have withheld.
        </p>
        <p>
          On failure: I have run blameless post-mortems for over a decade because they produce better information, not just because they are kinder. When the goal of an incident review is to understand the system, you get honest accounts of what actually happened. When the goal is to assign fault, you get defensive accounts designed to protect the narrator. Defensive accounts are useless for preventing the next incident. A culture that treats failure as information — while still distinguishing between systemic failures and genuine negligence — will out-learn one that treats failure as evidence of individual shortcoming, every time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Track Record Section -->
  <section class="bg-cream-dark py-16 md:py-24 px-4 md:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy font-sans mb-4">Track record</h2>
      <p class="text-slate text-lg mb-12 max-w-2xl">
        Almost three decades in software, data, and AI, from game development in Austria to data platforms across Europe and, more recently, building in Armenia. Alongside these roles I have lectured at UAS Technikum Wien and mentored founders through EPIC and the Armenian Engineers &amp; Scientists of America. The full history is on
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/stefanpapp" class="text-amber hover:underline" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        {roles.map((role) => (
          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-8 shadow-sm">
            <span class="inline-block font-sans text-sm font-semibold text-amber mb-3 tracking-wide uppercase">
              {role.timeframe}
            </span>
            <h3 class="font-sans font-bold text-xl text-navy mb-1">{role.title}</h3>
            <p class="font-sans text-slate text-sm font-medium mb-4">{role.org}</p>
            <p class="font-sans text-slate leading-relaxed">{role.description}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Testimonials Section -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 px-4 md:px-8 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy font-sans mb-4">What people say</h2>
      <p class="text-slate text-lg mb-12">
        Recommendations from people I have worked with. More are on
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/stefanpapp" class="text-amber hover:underline" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-12">
        {testimonials.map((t) => (
          <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-amber pl-6">
            <p class="font-serif text-lg text-slate leading-relaxed italic mb-4">{t.quote}</p>
            <footer class="font-sans text-sm text-slate/70">{t.attribution}</footer>
          </blockquote>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
